---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Evidence from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Evidence.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Method"
  eyebrow="How to read anything on this site"
  dek="Every claim here carries a confidence. The point of saying so is that a reader can disagree with the reasoning instead of having to take the conclusion on trust.">


  <section class="blk" id="ladder">
    <h2>The five words</h2>
    <p class="prose">All seven of these family archives grade a claim with the same five words, so a
    level means the same thing wherever you meet it. The word is always printed next to the colour,
    because a reader should never have to know the palette to know the confidence.</p>
    <dl class="ladder">
      <dt><Evidence level="documented" /></dt>
      <dd>A record was read, and it says this. The source is named.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="probable" /></dt>
      <dd>The evidence points one way and nothing found so far points the other. Not proven.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="inferred" /></dt>
      <dd>Reasoned from what is known — a gap of the right size, a name in the right place — rather
      than witnessed in a record.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="family" /></dt>
      <dd>Told, remembered, or written on a family chart. Kept, because discarding it would lose the
      lead; labelled, because it is not evidence.</dd>
      <dt><Evidence level="disputed" /></dt>
      <dd>Two sources disagree and neither has been ruled out. Both are shown.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class="notclaimed">
      <span class="lab">Not claimed</span>
      <p class="prose">Some pages carry this instead. It states the thing a reader is likely to
      assume — the connection that would be tidy, the identification that almost works — and says
      plainly that this archive does not assert it. An archive is judged as much by what it refuses
      to say as by what it says.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The five labels</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Documented</th><td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan. The
        strongest things in this archive are the <strong>FamilySearch citations into the Croatian
        parish registers</strong> — Senj, Krivi Put, Otočac, Karlobag — which give an image group,
        a page and a date.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Inferred</th><td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning
        written out so it can be overturned. That the family is <a href={u("/bunjevci/")}>Bunjevac</a>
        is an inference from place and surname, not a statement in any record.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Superseded</th><td>Asserted in the family record, and now displaced by a document that
        says otherwise.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Disputed</th><td>Asserted in the family record but unsupported, or contradicted, by
        what can be seen — like the <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>join at generation three</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Family lore</th><td>Told, remembered, not corroborated. Kept because it is precious;
        labelled because pretending otherwise is how family myths become family history. Both
        accounts of <a href={u("/oto/")}>Oto's death</a> sit here.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" id="living">
    <div class="lab">The rule that governs the whole build</div>
    <h2>A living person is named, and nothing more</h2>
    <p class="lede">A living person (80 or younger, with no death recorded) is named with their
    relationships and their town and country, and nothing more. No dates, no records, no sources, no
    photograph, no structured data. Their page is kept out of search engines and out of the sitemap,
    and so is a name page whose every bearer is living.</p>
    <p>The rule is the one every family archive shares, written once in the kit
    (<code class="mono">kit/tools/living_rule.py</code>) and used here through
    <code class="mono">tools/livingset.py</code>:</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Living</strong> is born 80 years ago or less, counted by year (this year minus the
        birth year), with no death or burial recorded.</li>
      <li><strong>A death or a burial in the tree</strong>, dated or not, means dead; so does a death
        read in <code class="mono">sources/documented.psv</code>, a grave photograph, or a surveyed stone
        that carries their name, birth year and a death year.</li>
      <li><strong>A public source</strong> (a citation from FamilySearch, a MyHeritage record
        collection, Findmypast and the others the kit lists) means the person is not treated as
        living. A private family-tree entry does not count.</li>
      <li><strong>Nobody with no birth year</strong> is living: it cannot be shown that they are 80
        or younger.</li>
      <li><strong>A child follows the same rule.</strong> A child with a known birth year is named like
        any living person.</li>
      <li><strong>Held out:</strong> an undated person (no birth year) with a living parent, who might
        be a child, is not published at all: no page and no name as anyone's relative. The rule is
        written down, by rule and never by person, in
        <code class="mono">sources/living-rule.json</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The generator (<code class="mono">tools/build_site_data.py</code>) never
    writes a living person's dates, events, notes, sources, occupations, burial or cause of death to any
    file the site reads, and a build gate (<code class="mono">check:living-standard</code>) recomputes who
    is living from the tree with the kit's rule, independently, and refuses the build if any of it breaks.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>The town and country</strong> are the birthplace in the tree, cut
    down to the town and the country: the county, the region, a street, an address, a hospital or a care
    home is dropped before anything is written. A residence or a census place of a living person is never
    read.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Every person this archive names has a page of their own</strong>,
    living or dead: a relative on somebody's family chart, or somebody tagged on a photograph, is a person
    with a page. It does not spread: a person who is published only because somebody named them lists only
    the relatives who already have pages.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and
    without requiring a reason.</strong></p>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem">Until 16 October 2026 this archive named none of its
    living people, drew them as a gap, and judged somebody living unless a century had passed. All the
    family archives now follow one standard, and this page says what this one does under it.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>An online family tree is a witness, not a source</h2>
    <p>The spine here is a MyHeritage tree of <strong>15,643 people</strong>. Most “sources” attached
    to people in it are <em>other trees</em> — <em>Geni World Family Tree</em>,
    <em>Boras Web Site</em>, <em>Grlica Web Site</em>, <em>Janicic</em>,
    <em>Marko Karic rodoslov</em>. When four trees agree, that is four witnesses who may all have
    copied one another; it is not four documents.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">So the register page marks only whether a person carries
    <em>any</em> source string at all, and every person page prints the strings verbatim so a reader
    can see what kind of thing is being relied on. Where a citation points at a
    <strong>parish register image</strong>, that is the one worth going to.
    <a href={u("/sources/")}>Sources →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Two things this archive does to the data, and admits to</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>It folds spellings</h3>
        <p>Žubrinić, Zubrinic, Zubrinich, Xubrinich and Xubrinić are counted as
        <strong>one name</strong>; so are Prpić and Perpić. Without that, the family fragments into
        eight small families that appear unrelated. The folding is done in one function so it can be
        inspected and argued with. <a href={u("/name/")}>Why the spellings differ →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>It drops the working scaffolding</h3>
        <p>The tree contains sorting stubs — <em>“Senj – Unknown To be Sorted Papić”</em>,
        <em>“Brothers [of Stephanus] Žubrinić”</em>, <em>“NN Blažević”</em>. These are research
        notes, not people, and publishing them as ancestors would be a lie. They are filtered by
        name pattern at the build boundary.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What is not committed</h2>
    <p class="note">The raw GEDCOM is <strong>not published</strong> — it contains every living
    person in full. It is kept out of any public copy of this repository and regenerated from a
    fresh MyHeritage export when needed.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
